PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Current consensus strongly favors A. Jack defaulted by missing the Feb. 1, 2023 payment. Gary’s Feb. 2 text likely counts as written notice under Ontario electronic commerce principles because it was received and informed Jack of the missed payment. Jack did not cure within the contract’s three-day period, so by Feb. 6 Gary had a contractual right to retake possession.

Colleagues split only on rationale: Colleague 6 chose E, arguing the transaction is not consumer-protected because Jack used the vehicle for business. This is the weakest point: Jack’s use was mixed—Subway commute, kids’ sports, and weekend handyman work—so business use does not clearly remove consumer status. In any event, E’s outcome matches A on default/notice.

Key supporting points: the “full agreement” clause cannot exclude applicable legislation, but no applicable statutory bar clearly prevents repossession here. The possible two-thirds consumer repossession protection does not help Jack because he paid only $1,500 of $3,000, i.e., one-half, below two-thirds. The private sale by Gary also makes Consumer Protection Act supplier-based rules unlikely to apply. Option C fails because a text can satisfy written notice. Option B fails because “substantial portion” is not the relevant standard and half payment is not enough. Option D fails because parties cannot contract out of legislation.

Unresolved/later-watch points: exact application of PPSA or Consumer Protection Act notice rules is not fully briefed, but no answer choice turns on a statutory notice period longer than the contract’s three days. The best answer remains A.
}

